**Applied Materials — integrated materials engineering.** Its approach can bring preparation, modification, deposition and etch operations into coordinated process solutions. On February 10, 2026, Applied announced gate-all-around tools addressing nanosheet treatment, precision trenches and lower-resistance contacts, and said multiple leading foundry-logic manufacturers were using them.

These companies are not necessarily bidding for the identical tool slot. They illustrate why a single node transition can create different opportunities: selective removal, precisely controlled growth and coordinated treatment of sensitive interfaces. Applied's announced customer use is stronger evidence than a mere product concept, but the release does not disclose comparative yields, revenue contribution or durable market share.

Why switching suppliers is difficult—but not impossible

After qualification, a fab has recipes, technicians, spare parts, maintenance procedures and years of defect-learning built around a tool. Replacing it means proving that the alternative works with upstream and downstream steps, not just matching one laboratory metric. Losing yield during the transition may cost more than the purchase-price saving.

This creates **switching costs**: the effort and risk of changing a supplier. But do not mistake qualification for permanent monopoly power. A rival can win at the next process generation, especially if its approach removes several steps, widens the process window or delivers lower cost per good wafer. Foundries also have incentives to qualify alternative suppliers where practical.

A practical buying test

Imagine Tool A processes 60 wafers per hour and Tool B processes 50. A looks 20% faster. Yet if B has fewer defects, better uptime or removes the need for another operation, it may deliver more good wafers per day at lower total cost. These are illustrative numbers, not vendor measurements.

The key questions for a fab are: does this process reliably make the intended structure; does it improve the *entire* sequence; can it operate at required volume; and how hard would switching become after qualification?

The same questions help explain vendor economics. A valuable equipment moat is built not merely on a patent or an advanced machine, but on a hard-to-replace process that repeatedly improves customers' usable output.

